/**

@page page_exagrype_library ExaGRyPE library

ExaGRyPE is Peano's extension for numerical relativity. It sits on top of
ExaHyPE 2 and hosts the C++ code that all ExaGRyPE applications share. The
applications themselves (such as \c applications/exahype2/ccz4, see
@ref applications_exahype2_ExaGRyPE) generate their solvers through the Python
API and link against this library.

# Configuration

ExaGRyPE is enabled like any other Peano extension or toolbox:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
./configure --enable-exahype --enable-blockstructured --enable-loadbalancing --enable-exagrype ...
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

or, with CMake,

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
cmake .. -DENABLE_EXAHYPE=ON -DENABLE_BLOCKSTRUCTURED=ON -DENABLE_LOADBALANCING=ON -DENABLE_EXAGRYPE=ON
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

ExaGRyPE requires ExaHyPE 2. As the Einstein equations are three-dimensional,
the library is built for 3d only, i.e. there is a \c libExaGRyPECore3d.a plus
its \c _debug, \c _asserts, \c _trace and \c _stats variants, but no 2d
counterpart. If enabled, configure adds the library to the 3d link line of all
generated Makefiles. Its unit tests run as part of \c UnitTests3d.

# Content

- exagrype::ccz4 holds the CCZ4 compute kernels (non-conservative product,
  source, eigenvalues, constraint enforcement and diagnostics) plus the
  reconstruction of the auxiliary variables for the fourth-order finite
  differences.
- exagrype::initialvalues holds analytical initial conditions such as the
  gauge wave or flat spacetime.

The TwoPunctures initial data for black holes is not part of the library yet,
as it requires GSL. It remains within \c applications/exahype2/ccz4 (see
\c ApplyTwoPunctures.h there).

*/
